  "client_view_text": " A write-ahead log (WAL) is a mechanism for maintaining a record of changes to a database before applying those changes to the main database file. Here's how it works:\n\nWhen a transaction is committed, the database first updates the write-ahead log with a record of the changes made by that transaction. This record includes information about the specific data that was modified, the type of modification (insert, update, delete), and possibly some additional metadata. The log is always kept in a stable storage device (such as a hard drive or solid-state drive) to prevent data loss.\n\nIn the event of a crash, the database can resume its operation from where it left off by replaying the write-ahead log. The database will go through the log entry by entry, applying the changes recorded in each entry to the main database file. This ensures that the database is in a consistent state, even if some transactions were not fully committed before the crash. If a transaction was interrupted before it could write its log entry, the database can simply skip over that incomplete transaction and continue with the next one.\n\nWAL provides a way to recover from crashes by ensuring that all modifications to the database are recorded before they are applied. This means that even if the main database file is damaged or corrupted,"
}